#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
bootchat2_web.py — BootChat web client + HTTP bridge

    python3 bootchat2_web.py
    python3 bootchat2_web.py --port 8080 --chat-host 127.0.0.1
    BOOTCHAT_PUBLIC_URL=https://boot.chat python3 bootchat2_web.py

Serves the BootChat browser client and bridges it to bootchat2_server.py.
Standard library only — nothing to install.

WHAT THIS IS

Each browser session gets its own real TCP connection to the chat server, so
the server sees a browser as an ordinary client: same LOGIN, same SAY, same
PING/PONG. The browser talks to this bridge over HTTP; the bridge is a dumb
pipe that moves protocol lines in both directions. All UI logic lives in the
page, so a protocol change is usually a page-only edit.

The bridge speaks no protocol of its own. It does not parse MSG, it does not
cache history, and it does not invent replies — if the chat server is down the
page says so rather than showing something that looks like data.

LONG POLLING

/api/poll blocks until a line arrives or POLL_WAIT seconds pass. The previous
version polled on a fixed 1s timer, which cost one request per second per open
tab whether or not anything had happened, and added up to a second of latency
to every message. Holding the request open removes both: idle tabs cost one
request every POLL_WAIT seconds, and a message is delivered the moment the
server emits it.

CONFIGURATION

Nothing about where the server lives belongs in the page. The browser talks to
this bridge on the same origin it was served from, and the bridge holds the
chat server address. That is what keeps a VPS address out of the user-facing
UI and lets the same build run in development and behind a real domain.

    BOOTCHAT_PUBLIC_URL    public origin, e.g. https://boot.chat
    BOOTCHAT_PUBLIC_NAME   what the sign-in screen shows (default: host of
                           PUBLIC_URL, or "HavenDOS ecosystem")
    BOOTCHAT_API_BASE      prefix for /api/* if the API is mounted elsewhere
    BOOTCHAT_CHAT_HOST     chat server host      (default 127.0.0.1)
    BOOTCHAT_CHAT_PORT     chat server port      (default 6700)
    BOOTCHAT_BIND          bind address          (default 0.0.0.0)
    BOOTCHAT_PORT          HTTP port             (default 8080)
    BOOTCHAT_BEHIND_PROXY  1 when nginx terminates TLS in front of this

Command-line flags override the environment; both override the defaults.

HTTPS / WSS — WHAT IS AND IS NOT TRUE HERE

The browser side is ready for TLS: put nginx or Cloudflare in front, set
BOOTCHAT_PUBLIC_URL to the https:// origin, and the page is served over HTTPS
with same-origin API calls. That is also what makes the service worker
register and what Web Push would require.

The HavenDOS side is NOT. shell/chat.c speaks the v2 protocol as raw TCP on
6700 with no transport encryption, because HavenDOS cannot do TLS for this
yet. Cloudflare Tunnel carries HTTP, not raw TCP, so it cannot serve the
HavenDOS client at all. Run that leg on a LAN or over Tailscale.

There is no WebSocket in this design, so there is no WSS to configure. HTTP
long polling was kept deliberately: it is standard-library only, it survives
proxies that mishandle upgrade headers, and at this scale it costs about the
same. If that changes, the page's transport is three functions.

SECURITY

Passwords travel from the browser to this bridge and on to the chat server.
Over HTTPS to the bridge that leg is encrypted; the bridge-to-server leg is
not, so keep them on the same host or a private network. A Content-Security-
Policy is sent that allows only this origin, which is what makes the page's
strict no-innerHTML rendering meaningful. Do not port-forward the raw chat
port to the internet.
"""

